Title: Business Requirements Document (BRD)
Project: Self-Hosted Data Analysis & Reporting Utility
Version: 1.0
Date: April 29, 2026

1. Executive Summary
The organization requires a self-hosted, single-tenant data analysis and reporting utility that integrates directly with PostgreSQL databases. The solution must provide manual business intelligence capabilities, API-first architecture, and optional AI-assisted features. The system must remain cost-efficient, relying solely on open-source components and user-provided LLM API usage.

2. Business Objectives
- Enable secure, self-hosted reporting within user infrastructure.
- Provide API-first access to all reporting functionality.
- Support manual BI operations (aggregations, joins, filters).
- Allow AI-assisted query/report generation using user-provided API keys.
- Ensure exportability of raw data and visualizations.
- Minimize custom development effort by leveraging open-source BI frameworks.

3. Scope
In-Scope:
- PostgreSQL integration.
- API-first reporting engine.
- Manual BI dashboards and queries.
- AI-assisted report generation.
- Export to CSV and structured reports.
Out-of-Scope:
- Multi-tenant hosting.
- Proprietary paid BI platforms.
- Non-PostgreSQL databases (future roadmap).

4. Stakeholders
- End Users: Analysts, business managers.
- IT Administrators: Responsible for deployment and maintenance.
- Developers: Responsible for orchestration and AI integration.

5. Success Criteria
- 90% of functionality delivered via open-source frameworks.
- ≤10% custom orchestration code.
- Secure, stable, and performant reporting engine.
- Seamless AI integration without replacing manual BI.


Title: Functional Requirements Document (FRD)
Project: Self-Hosted Data Analysis & Reporting Utility
Version: 1.0
Date: April 29, 2026

1. Core Functional Requirements
- API-First & Headless:
  - All reporting functionality exposed via REST/GraphQL APIs.
  - Optional built-in UI for dashboards.
- Manual BI:
  - Support for aggregations, group by, joins, date filters.
  - Query history tracking.
- AI Assist:
  - AI reads database schema/semantic layer.
  - AI suggests possible reports.
  - AI accepts natural language commands to configure/edit reports.
- Exportability:
  - Export raw data to CSV.
  - Export visualizations and structured reports (PDF, XLS).
- Security:
  - Credentials stored securely.
  - Single-tenant deployment ensures isolation.

2. Non-Functional Requirements
- Performance: Queries must execute within acceptable latency (<2s for standard aggregations).
- Scalability: Must handle datasets up to 100M rows.
- Reliability: 99.9% uptime target.
- Maintainability: Modular architecture with minimal custom code.
- Licensing: Only free/open-source components.

3. User Stories
- As an analyst, I want to run manual queries and export results to CSV.
- As a manager, I want to view dashboards with filters and aggregations.
- As a developer, I want to integrate reporting APIs into existing systems.
- As a user, I want AI to suggest reports based on schema metadata.


Title: Technical Architecture Document
Project: Self-Hosted Data Analysis & Reporting Utility
Version: 1.0
Date: April 29, 2026

1. System Architecture
PostgreSQL Database
   │
   ▼
Cube.dev (Semantic Layer + API-first Query Engine)
   │
   ├── Metabase OSS (Manual BI Dashboards, CSV Export)
   │
   └── JasperReports/DynamicJasper (Structured Report Generation)

2. Components
- Cube.dev:
  - Provides semantic layer.
  - Exposes REST/GraphQL APIs.
  - Schema metadata accessible to AI.
- Metabase OSS:
  - Provides UI dashboards.
  - Supports manual BI operations.
  - Exports CSV and tracks query history.
- JasperReports/DynamicJasper:
  - Generates structured, printable reports.
  - Consumes Cube queries for consistency.

3. AI Integration
- AI layer connects to Cube semantic schema.
- Natural language → Cube query translation.
- AI orchestrates report creation in Metabase or Jasper.

4. Deployment
- Self-hosted Docker containers.
- Single-tenant architecture.
- Secure credential injection for PostgreSQL.

5. Technology Stack
- Backend: Cube.dev (Node.js), Metabase (Clojure/Java), JasperReports (Java).
- Orchestration: Custom service (Python/Go/Node.js).
- AI Integration: OpenAI/Gemini API via orchestration layer.


Title: Autonomous Coding Specification
Project: Self-Hosted Data Analysis & Reporting Utility
Version: 1.0
Date: April 29, 2026

Objective:
Implement a self-hosted, single-tenant reporting utility that connects to PostgreSQL, exposes API-first reporting functionality, supports manual BI, integrates AI assistance, and enables exportability.

System Requirements:
- PostgreSQL integration with secure credential storage.
- Cube.dev as semantic layer and API-first query engine.
- Metabase OSS for manual BI dashboards and CSV export.
- JasperReports/DynamicJasper for structured report generation.
- Orchestration service to unify APIs and integrate AI.

Functional Modules:
1. Database Connector:
   - Connect to PostgreSQL using provided credentials.
   - Validate schema and expose metadata.
2. Semantic Layer (Cube.dev):
   - Define schema models.
   - Expose REST/GraphQL APIs for queries.
3. BI Dashboards (Metabase):
   - Provide manual query builder.
   - Support filters, joins, aggregations.
   - Export CSV and track query history.
4. Reporting Engine (JasperReports):
   - Generate structured reports (PDF, XLS).
   - Consume Cube queries for consistency.
5. AI Assist Layer:
   - Accept API keys for LLMs.
   - Read Cube schema metadata.
   - Translate natural language → Cube queries.
   - Suggest dashboards/reports.
6. Orchestration Service:
   - Written in Python/Node.js/Go.
   - Routes queries between Cube, Metabase, Jasper.
   - Provides unified API endpoint.
   - Handles AI integration.

Deployment:
- Dockerized containers for Cube.dev, Metabase, JasperReports, and orchestration service.
- Single-tenant deployment model.
- Environment variables for PostgreSQL credentials and LLM API keys.

Constraints:
- Only free/open-source components.
- Running costs limited to server hosting and user’s LLM API usage.
- ≤10% custom orchestration code.

Deliverables:
- Docker Compose file for deployment.
- Orchestration service source code.
- API documentation (OpenAPI/Swagger).
- Example AI integration scripts.

